  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Adding the 2 equations gives \\(2x=6\\), so \\(x=3\\). The y-coordinate is 2.\n\nAlternative method 1: Substitute y from the first equation\n\nThe first equation gives \\(y=5-x\\). Substituting this into the second equation leaves only \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx-(5-x)=1,\\qquad 2x=6.\n\\]\nThus \\(x=3\\), and then \\(y=5-3=2\\). The intersection is \\((3,2)\\), so its \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(3\\).",
